@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — two searches carried in the Sword of Moses
#
# Both were carried on 2026-09-24 with the work open and part three read
# through, and both came back empty. They are the two questions the holding
# most wants answered of a corpus of this kind, and the answer in both cases
# is a gap the work exhibits about itself: a hundred and thirty-six operations
# with no condition of failure between them, and a list that includes killing
# a man with no word against using it.
#
# The work stands in traditions/hekhalot.ttl, where four nodes from its
# opening instructions had been entered on the fourth of September.
#

pmp:HarbaDeMosheDefectusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether any of the hundred and thirty-six operations states a condition of failure"@en, "Ob eine der hundertsechsunddreißig Verrichtungen eine Misslingensbedingung nennt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Sword of Moses, part III in full, numbers 1 to 136 in the editor's numbering, ed. and trans. Moses Gaster (London: David Nutt, 1896)" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any sentence attached to any single operation saying what would show that it had not worked, what to do if it did not, or under what circumstance it does not hold: a term after which nothing more comes, a botched ingredient, an unfit operator, a repetition on failure. The work carries two stated consequences, one in its framing part and one at its close, and both are about the operator rather than the operation; the question is whether the operations themselves carry anything of the kind." ;
    pm:searchTerms "The whole of part three was read through continuously, and then searched with the text normalised to bare letters, for: if it does not (no hits); will not work (none); fail in any form (none at all); in vain (none); unless (none); does not succeed (none); if he has not (none); be of no avail (none); try again (none); nothing will (one hit, and it is a promise — say this in the fall and nothing will hurt thee); repeat it (one, and it is an instruction to repeat a remedy for three days, not on failure)." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-24" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-24" ;
    skos:note "A hundred and thirty-six operations and not one condition of failure. The form of the entries is uniform throughout: do this to this material, say the named section, and the thing follows. The verb is never conditional. Two entries do have two outcomes — the lettuce whose leaves close against the journey at number 65, and the sick man who turns his face towards the operator or away at number 90 — but both are questions put to the world and not tests of the operation, and in both the operation is held to have worked either way."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the gap the search exists to exhibit, and it is a large one. The holding's whole stage three rests on transmissions that say what would count against them, and this corpus, which is as operative as anything here and more specific about its materials than most, says nothing of the kind anywhere in its hundred and thirty-six entries. Whoever wants to know how many claims of this corpus are testable now has the number, and it is not an estimate."@en ;
    skos:note "It also settles what the sanction at pmc:HarbaDeMosheCaution is and is not. That sentence threatens the operator with strangling if he leaves a formula off an amulet; it says nothing about the amulet working or not working, and this search establishes that nothing else in the work does either."@en ;
    skos:note "The limit of the finding. Part two, the list of names the operations refer to, is not printed in this translation and could not be searched; if a condition of failure stands anywhere in the work it would have to stand there, among names, which is not where a condition of failure is put. The critical edition of 1997 rests on a fuller manuscript and was not obtainable in this run."@en .

pmp:HarbaDeMosheCautioSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the work anywhere warns against the operations that do harm"@en, "Ob das Werk irgendwo vor den schädigenden Verrichtungen warnt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Sword of Moses, the framing part and the whole of part III, ed. and trans. Moses Gaster (London: David Nutt, 1896)" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A caution, a restriction or a condition of any kind attached to the operations that injure: killing a man with an image and a bow, sending plagues, sending an evil spirit, making a woman miscarry, making a man ill, destroying a neighbour's house, making him disliked, taking a rich man's riches, making the bystanders kill one another. The question matters because the same work fences its own use with three days of purity, a secrecy rule and a prohibition on touching, and because the holding carries several transmissions in which the self-warning stands inside the prescribing passage." ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the translation, normalised to bare letters, for: take heed (one hit, and it is in the second recension printed as an appendix, not in this work, and it is about oaths and modesty); beware (no hits at all); do not use (none); forbidden (one, and it is the editor's introduction about the Tetragrammaton); sin (one, in a prayer of supplication); warn (two, one the editor on a responsum of Hai Gaon and one an optical misreading of was heard); not lawful (none). The harmful operations were then read again in place to see whether a caution stood beside any of them without those words." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-24" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-24" ;
    skos:note "Nothing anywhere. The operation for killing a man is set out with the same evenness as the one for a painful nerve, between an instruction for catching fish and one for sending plagues, and no sentence in the work distinguishes them. What the work does guard is its own handling: three days of purity, secrecy about the intention, a prohibition on touching before the preparations, and at the close a threat of strangulation for an amulet written without its opening formula."@en ;
    skos:note "That asymmetry is the finding and it is stated here rather than in a note on some node, because it is a property of the whole work and not of any one entry. The transmission takes great care about the operator's condition and none at all about what he does with it."@en ;
    skos:note "One search that was named and not carried, and it is the gap of this run rather than of the work. The rabbinic condemnation of magic — the Mishnah at Sanhedrin 7:11 with its distinction between the sorcerer who does a deed and the one who dazzles the eyes, the discussion at Bavli Sanhedrin 67a to 68a, and Shabbat 75a on learning from a magician — is the counter-transmission to this corpus, and public-domain English translations of all of it are reachable by the regular route: Danby's Tractate Sanhedrin of 1919 for the Mishnah and the Tosefta beside it, Rodkinson's Talmud for the rest. None of it was opened at the wording in this run, and no node here rests on it. It is the first thing the next run at this corpus should do, and it is cheap."@en .
